{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Three se'ahs.</b> These are an ephah, as our Rabbis said: 'an ephah is three se'ahs.' And since the way of Scripture is to be concise, it would have been fitting to say 'hurry, an ephah.' And the fact that it said 'three se'ahs' — it is possible to say that he intended honor for all three of them, and made equal portions so as not to introduce jealousy at the meal, and brought a se'ah before each and every one, to show that the honor of all three of them was equal in his eyes. And there is yet another intention: in order to increase the spectacle and to show that they were busy and exerting themselves in honors, so that the people of the place would know this; therefore he exerted himself and toiled in their honor, going out and coming in like one who exerts himself for the honor of Shabbat, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said: Rav Nahman would carry on his shoulder and enter, and carry on his shoulder and go out. He said: if Rabbi Ami and Rabbi Asi chanced upon me, I would not carry on my shoulder before them — and his intention was to say that the merit of Shabbat was not inferior in his eyes to the merit of those two sages distinguished in their generation, and since when they would enter to lodge with him he would toil and exert himself in their honor, all the more so should one exert himself in the honor of Shabbat at its entry. And furthermore, Scripture teaches us, by saying 'three se'ahs,' that her eye was generous — had it said 'an ephah,' it would have implied a limited ephah; therefore he elaborated and wrote 'three se'ahs,' to make clear that it was a large ephah, and he was bringing the flour with a good eye and with great generosity:"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:13:06.913481834Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}